The graph of $y = x + \dfrac{1}{x}$ has turning points at $(1, 2)$ and $(-1, -2)$, and a vertical asymptote at $x = 0$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4.5, xmax=4.5, ymin=-5.5, ymax=5.5, samples=120, clip=false, restrict y to domain=-5.5:5.5, unbounded coords=jump, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2,4}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4}]
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=0.2:4.2] {x + 1/x};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-4.2:-0.2] {x + 1/x};
\addplot[dashed, red, domain=-5.2:5.2] ({0}, {x});
\node[right] at (axis cs:0.2, 4.6) {$x=0$};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, blue] coordinates {(1,2) (-1,-2)};
\node[below right] at (axis cs:1,2) {$(1, 2)$};
\node[below left] at (axis cs:-1.1,-2.2) {$(-1, -2)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State its domain and range in interval notation.
